    "https://careers.uth.tmc.edu/us/en/job/260001BV/Vice-Dean-for-Academic-Affairs-Cizik-School-of-Nursing": {
      "title": "Vice Dean for Academic Affairs",
      "description": "Vice Dean for Academic Affairs - Cizik School of Nursing  in Texas Medical Center-Houston, Texas, United States | Faculty & Physicians at UTHealth Houston Skip to main content Employee Login External Login Sign up Employee Login External Login Start Your Search UT Physicians Faculty Executive Behavioral Sciences Campus Postdoctoral Research Fellow Internal Applicants Search All Why UTHealth Houston? Benefits Career Development and Recognition Awards & Accolades Join Our Talent Community Veterans Blog Start Your Search UT Physicians Faculty Executive Behavioral Sciences Campus Postdoctoral Research Fellow Internal Applicants Search All Why UTHealth Houston? Benefits Career Development and Recognition Awards & Accolades Join Our Talent Community Veterans Blog Saved jobs (0) - We use cookies to offer you a better browsing experience, analyze site traffic, and personalize content. Read about how we use cookies and how you can control them by visiting our Cookie Settings page. If you continue to use this site, you consent to our use of cookies. Cookie Settings Deny Allow Vice Dean for Academic Affairs - Cizik School of Nursing Texas Medical Center-Houston, Texas Category Faculty & Physicians Job Id 260001BV Hybrid Save Apply Now Back to search results Previous job Next job JOB DESCRIPTION Vice Dean for Academic Affairs Cizik School of Nursing Position Summary: The Cizik School of Nursing at UTHealth Houston seeks a dynamic and visionary leader to serve as Vice Dean for Academic Affairs. In close partnership with the Dean, this role is instrumental in advancing a culture of scholarship and excellence across both educational and clinical programs. The Vice Dean provides strategic leadership and oversight for the planning, implementation, and continuous evaluation of innovative academic and clinical programs. Collaborating with multidisciplinary clinical partners and stakeholders across the broader healthcare community, this leader promotes excellence in education, clinical practice, and institutional development. This role plays a critical part in shaping the future of nursing education by fostering an environment grounded in evidence-based practice, academic rigor, and meaningful collaboration. The Vice Dean is also expected to serve as an engaged faculty member, fulfilling teaching, scholarship, and service expectations commensurate with rank and tenure. Cizik School of Nursing at UTHealth Houston is guided by a commitment to excellence in education, clinical practice, and scientific discovery. As one of seven schools within UTHealth Houston, it offers highly ranked, affordable programs and fosters interdisciplinary collaboration across the Texas Medical Center and beyond. The school provides a full continuum of programs, including BSN, MSN, DNP, PhD, and post-graduate offerings. Graduate students can pursue specialized tracks such as Nursing Leadership, Family Nurse Practitioner (FNP), Adult/Gerontology Acute Care Nurse Practitioner (AGACNP), Psychiatric/Mental Health Nurse Practitioner (PMHNP), Nurse Anesthesia (CRNA), Emergency Nurse Practitioner (ENP), and the nation’s first Emergency Medical Services Nurse Practitioner (EMSNP) subspecialty. Pipeline initiative includes the Pre-Nursing Pathway, summer healthcare certification programs, and SHPEP—supporting early access and student development. Research Excellence Cizik is nationally recognized for its research impact, ranking #1 in Texas and #8 in the nation in NIH-funded nursing research (Blue Ridge Institute for Medical Research, 2025). Faculty lead innovative work in areas such as stroke, dementia caregiving, HIV prevention, symptom science, pain management, and health disparities. The school is also home to the Cizik Nursing Research Institute (CNRI) , which provides advanced infrastructure, mentorship, and cutting-edge facilities, including the Smart Apartment for aging-in-place research and the Biobehavioral Health Laboratory. Campus & Learning Environment Cizik School of Nursing at UTHealth Houston is housed in a state-of-the-art, 194,000-square-foot facility designed to support nursing education, research, and student engagement. Located adjacent to Grant Fay Park within the Texas Medical Center, the eight-story building offers modern classrooms, research laboratories, a 200-seat auditorium, collaborative student spaces, and administrative offices. Designed with a focus on sustainability and wellness, the LEED® Gold-certified facility incorporates natural light, enhanced indoor air quality, and environmentally responsible features such as energy-efficient systems, recycled materials, and rainwater harvesting. The campus is further enhanced by the Simulation and Clinical Performance Laboratory (Sim Lab) , a 27,000-square-foot, advanced training environment. Equipped with high-fidelity manikins, virtual reality technology, and flexible simulation spaces, the Sim Lab prepares more than 2,000 students annually to manage complex clinical scenarios across diverse care settings, from home health to intensive care. A centralized control system lets faculty guide real-time learning and evaluation in an immersive environment. The ideal candidate will be at the Associate or Full Professor level. Candidates must be eligible for RN licensure in Texas and hold a doctoral degree (PhD in Nursing or a related field such as DrPH), with a demonstrated commitment to education, faculty development, research, and clinical practice. For confidential inquiries or to nominate a colleague, please contact the Office of Executive Recruitment at Shalan.e.Mitchell@uth.tmc.edu or 713-500-3251. Position Accountabilities: · Provide visionary leadership in meeting the academic and clinical goals of the School of Nursing through strategic planning and monitoring outcomes consistent with academic mission and scholarship. · Monitor cost and effectiveness of academic programs at the School of Nursing. · Serve in the role of dean of students for administration of applicable policies. · Oversee operations of Student Affairs Office as an effective and efficient student service liaison. · Collaborate with department chairs and educational program leaders in monitoring teaching assignments and faculty workload. · Serve as a liaison with educational, clinical and community units within and beyond the University. · Submit timely reports essential for accreditation, Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education, and outcomes reporting. · Approve training and project grants related to academic programs. · Promote the visibility of the School of Nursing within an interdisciplinary model to the University and beyond. · Maintain process for without salary faculty appointments in coordination with department chairs. · Review performance, counsel, recommend salary, and human resources issues for direct reports. · Serve as an advisor to the Dean on academic, clinical, community and international issues. · Represent the Dean in his/her absence. · Represent the Dean and School of Nursing as needed at local, state, and national organizations, events, and meetings. · 504 Coordinator for CSON. Minimum Experience Current RN licensure in Texas (or eligibility for licensure) and a doctoral degree in Nursing or a related field (e.g., PhD, DrPH) Minimum Education Five years experience as a collegiate administrator preferred Apply Now Save If you are looking for a great healthcare career in Houston, visit http://go.uth.edu/careers ! University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston (UTHealth Houston) Established in 1972 by The University of Texas System Board of Regents, The University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston (UTHealth Houston) is Houston’s Health University and Texas’ resource for health care education, innovation, scientific discovery and excellence in patient care. The most comprehensive academic health center in the UT System and the U.S. Gulf Coast region, UTHealth Houston is home to schools of biomedical informatics, biomedical sciences, dentistry, nursing and public health and the John P. and Kathrine G. McGovern Medical School. UTHealth Houston includes The University of Texas Harris County Psychiatric Center, as well as the growing clinical practices UT Physicians, UT Dentists and UT Health Services. The university’s primary teaching hospitals are Memorial Hermann-Texas Medical Center, Children’s Memorial Hermann Hospital and Harris Health Lyndon B. Johnson Hospital. UTHealth Houston Benefits UTHealth Houston offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package. For more information on our benefits programs please refer to our benefits page . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement UTHealth Houston is committed to providing equal opportunity in all employment-related activities without regard to race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions), gender, sexual orientation, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, gender identity or expression, veteran status, or any other basis prohibited by law or University policy. Reasonable accommodation, based on disability or religious observances, will be considered in accordance with applicable law and UTHealth Houston policy. All UTHealth Houston employees who are assigned to work at a location that is subject to the affiliated partner’s hospital, clinical offices, or agency are required to abide by UTHealth Houston’s rules and regulations, as well as the affiliate’s rules and regulations. In accordance with Executive Order GA-48 and to the extent that this position requires you to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Texas Business and Commerce Code §117.001(2), this role will include routine reviews to determine whether things such as criminal history or continuous connections to the government or political apparatus of a foreign adversary might prevent you from being able to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastruct